How it works
1. Compare each pair of adjacent numbers.
2. Swap them if the left number is larger.
3. Repeat the comparisons. After each pass, the largest unsorted number moves to the end.
4. Stop early if a pass makes no swaps.
The bubbleSort function sorts the slice in place, prints each pass, and returns the number of passes. For {5, 2, 8, 1, 4}, the result is {1, 2, 4, 5, 8}.
The worst-case time complexity is O(n²). With the early-stop optimization, already sorted data takes just one pass.
